    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530447/tenure-track-assistant-professor-in-transportation-engineering": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Transportation Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Transportation Engineering Apply now Job no: 530447 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Salary: Anticipated salary range of $115,000-$122,000 Applications Open: Nov 04 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Transportation Engineering, beginning in Fall 2027. We seek an innovative teacher-scholar who will establish a nationally recognized, externally funded research program integrating transportation engineering with artificial intelligence (AI), advanced computing, optimization, control, and robotics to advance the analysis, design, and operation of safe, sustainable, resilient, and equitable transportation systems. The successful candidate will also be an outstanding and dedicated educator who is committed to evidence-based teaching, student mentoring, and the success of learners from diverse backgrounds, and who will contribute enthusiastically to our collaborative, inclusive, and student-centered academic community. We welcome candidates with a strong and forward-looking research agenda in one or more of the following areas: • Connected and automated mobility. Multimodal sensing and perception, including vision-language models; prediction of road-user behavior; decision-making, trajectory planning, and control of automated vehicles; cooperative driving and interactions with human drivers and other road users; and safety and reliability under uncertainty. • Traffic flow and network control. Traffic flow theory and modeling; optimization and control of mixed traffic involving human-driven and automated vehicles; traffic signal control, corridor management, and network-wide operations; and links between vehicle-level decisions and system-level traffic performance. • AI and optimization for transportation and logistics systems. AI-enabled modeling, optimization, and control of passenger transportation, freight networks, and logistics operations to improve efficiency, reliability, resilience, and sustainability. Topics include network disruption modeling and disaster response and recovery, energy efficiency and emissions reduction, autonomous trucking and last-mile delivery, and warehouse robotics and intralogistics. • Intelligent infrastructure for future mobility. Planning, design, and management of physical transportation infrastructure and digital systems to support connected, automated, and electric mobility. Topics include transportation digital twins, roadside sensing and cooperative perception, electric vehicle charging networks and power-grid integration, and AI-enabled infrastructure monitoring and asset management to improve safety, reliability, and resilience. We value research that advances transportation science and engineering through original theoretical insights, innovative computational methods, or novel system-level solutions. Interdisciplinary approaches grounded in transportation engineering are encouraged. About UMass Lowell, the Francis College of Engineering, and the City of Lowell UMass Lowell is a Carnegie R1 public research university located approximately 25 miles northwest of Boston, offering an engaging setting for faculty who value collaboration, student learning, and real-world impact. The University’s talented and diverse students benefit from hands-on educational experiences, strong connections with industry and government, and opportunities to participate in meaningful research and community-based projects. The Francis College of Engineering supports fundamental and applied research across engineering, computing, and related fields, with opportunities for collaboration through the New England Robotics Validation and Experimentation (NERVE) Center, the Center for AI Computing Research (AICORE), and the Lowell Innovation Network Corridor (LINC). Together, these resources create a welcoming, interdisciplinary environment in which faculty can build innovative research programs, mentor students, and develop partnerships throughout the Greater Boston region. Situated along the Merrimack River, Lowell combines a rich industrial heritage with a vibrant arts scene, historic neighborhoods, and the Lowell National Historical Park. Its commuter-rail connection to Boston and proximity to the coast and mountains of New England offer access to cultural attractions and outdoor recreation. The successful candidate will establish an independent, externally funded research program, publish high-quality scholarship, and advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students. Teaching responsibilities will include core transportation engineering courses, such as traffic engineering, traffic flow theory, and transportation operations and control, as well as courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. The candidate will collaborate across disciplines, work with transportation agencies and industry partners, and contribute to departmental, university, and professional service. Minimum Qualifications (Required): A Ph.D. in transportation engineering, civil engineering, industrial engineering, systems engineering, or a closely related field by the appointment start date, with research expertise relevant to transportation engineering. A record of scholarly achievement appropriate to career stage and strong potential to establish an independent, externally funded research program. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach core transportation engineering courses, mentor students, and communicate effectively in an interdisciplinary academic environment. A collaborative approach to research, teaching, and service, along with the ability to contribute positively and thrive in a diverse academic community. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated research accomplishments or potential for significant contributions in one or more of the identified research areas. Experience or potential to develop research partnerships with public agencies, industry, and interdisciplinary teams. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited and in the MTA/Mass. Society of Professors union. Although the union contract minimum is set at $75,000, we anticipate a hiring range of $115,000 - $122,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following required documents with your application: A cover letter describing qualifications, research and teaching interests, and fit with the position. A curriculum vitae and up to three representative publications or research products. A research statement outlining past contributions, future research directions, and plans to pursue external funding. A teaching statement describing teaching experience and interests, including preparation to teach core transportation engineering courses and potential advanced or interdisciplinary offerings. Names and contact information of three professional references will be required during the application process. All materials should be submitted electronically through the UMass Lowell employment portal. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530457/assistant-teaching-professor-philosophy-ai-and-technology-ethics": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Philosophy (AI and Technology Ethics)",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Philosophy (AI and Technology Ethics) Apply now Job no: 530457 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Philosophy Salary: See posting section for anticipated salary range details. Applications Open: Nov 04 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The Department of Philosophy at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for a full-time, promotion-eligible position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor, beginning in the 2027–28 academic year. The position is part of a university-hiring initiative focused on artificial intelligence and emerging technologies. We seek a collaborative educator with expertise in the ethical, social, and philosophical dimensions of artificial intelligence and emerging technologies. This is a teaching and service-focused position with no research expectations. The successful candidate will teach three undergraduate courses per semester, with areas of teaching competence in Engineering Ethics, AI Ethics, Philosophy of AI, and Introduction to Ethics, and will undertake significant service responsibilities within the Department and across the University. The successful candidate will contribute to interdisciplinary teaching and curriculum development at the intersection of ethics, technology, and society. This work will build on the Department's existing strengths in social and political philosophy, philosophy of science, and American pragmatism, as well as the University's strengths in engineering, computing, and technological innovation. The successful candidate will also contribute to university-wide efforts to integrate AI into the curriculum, working with faculty and academic leaders, including the University's Director of AI Learning & Career Innovation. The University of Massachusetts Lowell is an R1 research university offering a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. The Department of Philosophy is a collegial department committed to undergraduate teaching and to making philosophical inquiry accessible to students from a wide range of backgrounds and disciplines. In addition to offering a major and minor in philosophy, the Department provides courses that fulfill general education and degree requirements for students across the University's colleges. Faculty members work in a range of philosophical traditions and areas of specialization, with particular strengths in social and political philosophy, philosophy of science, and American pragmatism. Philosophy is one of 12 academic departments and schools in the University's College of Fine Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences. Located approximately 25 miles northwest of Boston, Lowell is connected to the city by commuter rail and offers access to New England's mountains, beaches, and cultural resources. Additional information about the Department is available at https://www.uml.edu/fahss/philosophy/ . Essential Job Duties: Service is a central component of this position, with an emphasis on curriculum development, interdisciplinary collaboration, and student support. Responsibilities will include: Developing ethics curricula and programming addressing AI, engineering, technology, public policy, and sustainability, including courses in AI and technology ethics. Collaborating with faculty and programs across the College of Fine Arts, Humanities, & Social Sciences, the Francis College of Engineering, and the Kennedy College of Computer Science to advance interdisciplinary ethics education. Contributing to institutional initiatives concerning AI governance and the responsible use of emerging technologies. Supporting ethics curriculum development and assessment and the Department's ongoing efforts to strengthen its undergraduate educational mission. Developing and organizing applied learning opportunities connecting philosophical inquiry to practical issues in AI, engineering, and technology. Advising philosophy majors and minors and coordinating the Department's student advising and graduation tracking. There may also be opportunities to work with graduate students in related disciplines. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Ph.D. in Philosophy from an accredited institution. Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate teaching, including experience teaching students from diverse backgrounds. Demonstrated commitment to service at the departmental, university, and/or community level. Additional Considerations: The College is strongly committed to diversity. Women, members of underrepresented groups, and scholars with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salary of $75,000 for Assistant Teaching Professor. However, our anticipated expected minimum salary is expected to be $85,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on qualifications and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $95,000. Initial review of applications will begin December 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. The position may close once an adequate number of qualified candidates have been received. Please include the following required documents with your application: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position Statement of teaching philosophy Student evaluations of teaching Statement of inclusivity Three letters of reference addressing the candidate's teaching effectiveness, instructional skills, and commitment to student learning will be required during the application process Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530444/faculty-parttime-performance": {
      "title": "Faculty Part-time (Performance)",
      "description": "Faculty Part-time (Performance) Apply now Job no: 530444 Position type: Faculty Part Time/Adjunct Benefit Status: Non-Benefited - Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Music Salary: See posting section for salary details for adjunct faculty Applications Open: Nov 04 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled The Department of Music at UMass Lowell is looking to hire an adjunct instructor for our Mindfulness and Optimal Performance course for the spring 2027 semester.. Candidates with experience in musician’s health and performance optimization are encouraged to apply. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Bachelor’s degree in music, or equivalent professional experience College Teaching experience or professional experience Preferred Qualifications: Graduate degree in Music Expertise in one or more areas of performance Special Instructions to Applicants: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. This is a part-time, non-benefited, Union of Adjunct Faculty, Local 1596, UAW position. For Adjunct Faculty salaries, please refer to the Adjunct Faculty salary chart for the rates. Please include a CV, cover letter and Reference Letters with your application. Names and contact information of three references will be required during the application process. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530456/assistant-teaching-professor-finance": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Finance",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor - Finance Apply now Job no: 530456 Position type: Faculty Full Time Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Finance Applications Open: Nov 03 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The Finance Department at the Robert J. Manning School of Business, University of Massachusetts Lowell, is accepting applications for a non-tenure track position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor to start late January 2027. The position is renewable annually, potentially leading to an appointment of Associate Teaching Professor following five consecutive years of excellent performance evaluations. The Manning School of Business boasts a vibrant community of approximately 4,000 undergraduate, master’s, doctoral, and grad certificate students and holds accreditation from AACSB International. Situated in a modern facility equipped with cutting-edge classrooms and a dedicated Trading Room housing 20 Bloomberg terminals, the school offers an unparalleled learning environment. Nestled just 30 miles northwest of Boston, UMass Lowell, with its expansive campus, serves as the second-largest public university in Massachusetts, catering to over 18,000 students. UMass Lowell is classified as a Carnegie Research 1 (R1) university. The Finance Department offers Ph.D. and MS degrees in Finance, a Finance concentration in the MBA, and a Finance concentration in the BSBA program. The incoming candidate would be expected to teach three courses per semester and to engage in substantial departmental service. Service could include student advising, mentoring, and recruiting, supporting undergraduate co-curricular activities, and managing the trading room. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in finance or MBA with a finance concentration from an AACSB accredited school The ability to work effectively in teams A record of high-quality teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels Excellent interpersonal and communication skills P re f e rred Qualifications: Ph.D. or DBA degree in finance from an AACSB accredited school Ability to teach a wide variety of finance classes at the graduate and undergraduate level Ability to advise students, supervise honors projects, and mentor student clubs Experience in managing a Bloomberg terminal-based finance lab Ability to integrate AI tools and data analysis skills (such as Python or advanced Excel) into finance courses Professional experience relevant to finance Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salary of $75,000 for Assistant Teaching Professor. However, our anticipated expected minimum salary is expected to be $110,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on qualifications and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $122,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Please contact the University of Massachusetts Lowell Human Resources Department at: https://www.uml.edu/hr/askhr.aspx for information regarding benefits and related employment matters or if you have any questions about the position. Please include the following required documents with your application: Current curriculum vitae Cover letter detailing accomplishments; teaching interests, experience and philosophy; and professional background Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., student course evaluations, department chair reviews, teaching awards) Names and contact information for three references. All materials should be submitted electronically through the UMass Lowell employment portal. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530442/open-rank-tenure-track-faculty-position-nuclear-science": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position - Nuclear Science",
      "description": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position - Nuclear Science Apply now Job no: 530442 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Physics and Applied Physics Applications Open: Oct 30 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The University of Massachusetts Lowell (UMass Lowell) invites applications for a full-time, open-rank, tenure-track/tenured faculty position in the Department of Physics and Applied Physics beginning in September 2027. We seek an innovative scholar conducting high-impact research in nuclear science, broadly defined. The successful candidate will be expected to establish an externally funded research program that strengthens and complements current research in the Department of Physics and Applied Physics and fosters collaboration across the university, including in interdisciplinary research. Preference will be given to candidates whose research programs will make substantive use of UMass Lowell’s nuclear and radiation research facilities, whether independently or in collaboration with existing programs across the university. UMass Lowell’s Radiation Laboratory provides substantial infrastructure for nuclear research, including a 1-MW research reactor, 5.5-MV Van de Graaff accelerator, a dedicated radiochemistry laboratory, and facilities for radiation measurement and gamma and neutron irradiation. These facilities also offer opportunities for hands-on undergraduate and graduate research and training. Candidates should describe how their proposed research and educational activities would draw on these resources, where relevant, and how their work would advance the department’s broader research and educational goals. UMass Lowell has longstanding strengths and growing programs in nuclear science and applied physics. The Department of Physics and Applied Physics, within the Kennedy College of Sciences, offers undergraduate degrees in Physics and Engineering Physics, M.S. and Ph.D. degrees in Physics with options in Radiological Sciences (ABET-accredited) and Medical Physics (CAMPEP-accredited). Our faculty conduct experimental, computational, and theoretical research across fundamental and applied physics, including nuclear physics, radiation detection and measurement, radiological health physics, medical physics, materials and condensed matter physics, optics and photonics, quantum information, space science, and astrophysics. The department also offers opportunities to develop or expand research in nuclear chemistry. Research groups collaborate across the department and the university, including with Nuclear Engineering, as well as with national laboratories, research hospitals, industry, government agencies, and other academic institutions. The department has a strong record of externally funded research and provides students with opportunities to participate directly in faculty research through close mentoring and hands-on training. UMass Lowell is a nationally recognized Carnegie R1 public research university experiencing strong and sustained growth. The Wall Street Journal ranks UMass Lowell as Massachusetts' top public university and the best public institution in the state for both 'Best Value' and social mobility. The university's rapid ascent reflects its commitment to research excellence, student success, and impactful partnerships across industry, government, and the community. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and sustain an externally funded research program, advise undergraduate and graduate students, and contribute to collaborative research across the university. A strong commitment to teaching and mentoring is essential, including the ability to contribute to core undergraduate Physics courses and graduate teaching across the Department of Physics and Applied Physics. Appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor will require a strong record of research, teaching, mentorship, and professional accomplishment commensurate with the requirements for that rank. Minimum Qualifications (Required): A PhD or equivalent degree in Physics, Nuclear Science, Radiochemistry, Nuclear Chemistry, Radiological Sciences, Medical Physics, or a closely related field. A strong record of research appropriate to the candidate's career stage and demonstrated potential to establish and sustain an externally funded research program. Demonstrated potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentorship. Interest in collaborative and interdisciplinary research. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salaries of $75,000 for Assistant Professor, $85,000 for Associate Professor, and $100,000 for Professor. However, our anticipated expected minimum salary (for Assistant Professor) is expected to be $105,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on rank, qualifications, and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $230,000. Initial review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026. Applications received by this date will receive full consideration. Review will continue until the position is filled. Please include the following required documents with your application: Curriculum Vitae (CV), including list of publications and research grants Cover Letter Teaching Statement/Philosophy Research Statement Names and contact information for three professional references Applicants are encouraged to discuss in their Research Statement potential collaborations with existing departmental research groups and, where relevant, opportunities to utilize or collaborate with UMass Lowell's Radiation Laboratory facilities. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530469/assistant-teaching-professor-in-clinical-applied-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Clinical & Applied Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Clinical & Applied Psychology Apply now Job no: 530469 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Psychology Salary: See posting section for anticipated hiring salary range. Applications Open: Nov 09 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The Department of Psychology at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor position in Clinical & Applied Psychology to begin September 1, 2027. We welcome applicants with expertise in clinical psychology or related applied areas, including behavior analysis, health psychology, social work, counseling psychology, and community-based practice, who can contribute to undergraduate and graduate education through teaching, mentorship, supervision, and experiential learning. We are particularly interested in applicants with expertise in one or more of the following areas: mental health and wellbeing, behavioral interventions, behavioral medicine, health promotion, clinical and community-based practice, and the prevention or treatment of psychological and behavioral health concerns. Additional areas of interest include interventions addressing mental and physical health, health disparities, substance use, chronic health conditions, and the needs of underserved populations. Applicants with other areas of applied expertise that complement the Department's existing strengths and curricular needs are also encouraged to apply. Applicants should demonstrate a strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate teaching, student mentorship, and experiential learning. We are especially interested in candidates with experience in clinical, healthcare, community, or social service settings who can connect psychological science with professional practice. The successful candidate will teach courses in their areas of expertise and may contribute to student supervision and experiential learning through community service learning, directed studies, honors theses, graduate fieldwork, and master's projects or theses. Opportunities may also include developing and coordinating applied training experiences with community organizations, healthcare providers, and other professional settings. We strongly encourage applications from candidates from backgrounds underrepresented in higher education. For a description of course offerings, please visit: https://www.uml.edu/Catalog/Undergraduate/FAHSS/Departments/Psychology/course-listing.aspx In addition to teaching, Teaching Professors are responsible for providing service to their department and the university. Department service activities include advising undergraduate and graduate students, coordinating experiential opportunities, coordinating programs, participating on a variety of department committees, and activities related to recruitment and retention. Teaching Professors are highly valued full-time faculty members who have full voting rights on faculty committees and opportunities for committee leadership. This is a nine-month appointment that is renewable annually. Assistant Teaching Professors are eligible for promotion to Associate Teaching Professor after completing six years of full-time service at UMass Lowell, with subsequent eligibility for promotion to Full Teaching Professor after six years of excellent performance at the Associate Teaching Professor rank. Teaching faculty are eligible for professional development support. Beyond the regular nine-month appointment, opportunities exist for additional compensation through teaching and advising during the summer months. About UMass Lowell The University of Massachusetts Lowell is a top-ranking R1 research university that offers an excellent salary and benefits package. The Department of Psychology is one of the largest departments on campus, with an on-campus and online B.A., a master's program in Applied Behavior Analysis and Autism Studies, and a doctoral program in Psychology. Psychology is one of 12 academic departments and schools in the University's College of Fine Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences (FAHSS), located 25 miles northwest of Boston and within two hours of mountains and beaches. The University provides opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across programs in psychology, public health, nursing, and related disciplines, as well as connections with healthcare and community organizations throughout the region. Additional information about the Department is available at www.uml.edu/psychology . Minimum Qualifications (Required): An earned doctoral degree in Psychology, Social Work, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Evidence of effective teaching or demonstrated potential for teaching excellence at the undergraduate or graduate level. Demonstrated expertise in clinical psychology or a related applied area relevant to the Department's instructional needs. Ability to contribute to student advising, mentorship, and experiential learning. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching undergraduate or graduate courses in clinical psychology, health psychology, behavior analysis, mental health, or related applied areas. Professional experience in clinical, healthcare, behavioral health, community-based, or social service settings. Experience supervising or mentoring students in applied research, clinical fieldwork, practicum, internships, or other experiential learning activities. Experience developing or maintaining partnerships with healthcare organizations, community agencies, or other applied training settings. Experience supporting students from diverse educational and personal backgrounds. Relevant professional licensure, certification, or credentials in clinical psychology, social work, behavior analysis, or related fields. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salary of $75,000 for Assistant Teaching Professor. However, our anticipated expected minimum salary is expected to be $85,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on qualifications, and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $100,000 Initial review of applications will begin on January 9, 2027, and continue until the position is filled; however, the posting may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following required documents with your application: Cover letter describing relevant professional experience and record of accomplishments. Curriculum vitae. Teaching statement/philosophy, including a description of previous teaching experience (no more than 2 pages). Evidence of excellence in teaching (e.g., evaluations, syllabi, rubrics, program/curriculum development). Names and contact information of three professional references will be required during the application process. All materials should be submitted electronically through the UMass Lowell employment portal. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530471/assistant-teaching-professor-of-education": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Education",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Education Apply now Job no: 530471 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: School of Education Salary: See posting section for anticipated salary range. Applications Open: Nov 10 2026 Applications Close: Nov 24 2026 The School of Education at the University of Massachusetts Lowell is seeking an equity-minded, collaborative colleague to fill an Assistant Teaching Professor position primarily supporting its expanding online Ed.D. Leadership in Schooling program. The successful candidate will contribute to the preparation and professional development of educational leaders through high-quality teaching, doctoral mentoring, program development, and service. Depending on program needs and the candidate’s expertise, the successful candidate may also contribute to teaching and other instructional activities in the School of Education’s undergraduate Bachelor of Education (BAEd) program. The Ed.D. is a practitioner-based program designed to support the ongoing professional needs of educational leaders in PK–12 contexts. The program is anchored in the belief that educational leaders can make a substantial impact on students, families, schools, and communities by integrating professional knowledge, practical experience, research evidence, and systematic inquiry. The School of Education at the University of Massachusetts Lowell prepares educators, educational leaders, and researchers through undergraduate, master’s, doctoral, and professional learning programs. The School of Education faculty are engaged in teaching, scholarship, program development, and partnerships with schools and communities. Faculty collaborate to prepare educators and educational leaders who can use research, evidence, professional knowledge, and reflective practice to improve educational opportunities and outcomes. The School values collaboration, excellence, inclusion, service, and strong partnerships with educational communities. We are seeking an individual who has: A demonstrated commitment to developing PK–12 school leaders who strive to improve educational opportunities for diverse and historically underserved communities; Demonstrated expertise in teaching PK–12 school leadership courses in one or more of the following areas: improvement science or related approaches to continuous improvement, and culturally responsive and anti-racist pedagogy; A record of supporting and supervising doctoral research that promotes continuous school improvement and social justice; Demonstrated experience in quantitative or mixed-methods research; Excellent teaching and communication skills. Essential Job Duties: This position is designed to support the continued growth and improvement of the Ed.D. Leadership in Schooling program. Responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Teaching graduate-level courses in the Ed.D. Leadership in Schooling program, including asynchronous online courses offered during fall, spring, and/or summer terms. Developing, revising, and delivering high-quality course materials and learning experiences consistent with approved program curriculum. Chairing and/or serving on dissertation-in-practice committees, reviewing student assessments and qualifying papers. Mentoring and advising student cohorts, as needed. Participating in the program’s annual week-long summer on-campus residency. Providing program coordination support, including collaborating with the program coordinator and faculty on program meetings, student progress, and program activities. Supporting curriculum development and continuous improvement of the Ed.D. program. Contributing to program recruitment, assessment, evaluation, and other activities that support program quality and growth. As program needs arise, teaching undergraduate courses and contributing to instructional activities in the BAEd program in areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and professional experience. Participating in School of Education faculty meetings, program meetings, committees, and other service activities as appropriate. Collaborating effectively with faculty, staff, students, PK–12 educators, and educational partners. Minimum Qualifications (Required): An earned Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Educational Leadership, Education Policy, Organizational Leadership, Curriculum and Instruction, or a closely related field by the start of the appointment. Experience teaching or mentoring adult learners. Experience leading or working with leadership in the PK-12 context. Demonstrated expertise in educational leadership and school improvement. Experience with improvement science and related approaches to continuous improvement, including action research, collaborative inquiry, design-based approaches, or Plan–Do–Study–Act (PDSA) cycles, preferably within a CPED-informed, cohort-based doctoral program and in supporting equity-focused problems of practice in students’ own PK–12 settings. Ability to participate in on-campus School of Education faculty events, residency activities, and meetings. Preferred Qualifications: Experience delivering online courses at the post-undergraduate or graduate level. Experience mentoring doctoral students and supporting practitioner-oriented doctoral research. Experience chairing, serving on, or otherwise supporting dissertation-in-practice committees. Experience as a PK–12 school or district leader. Experience in culturally relevant/anti-racist pedagogy. Experience in supervision of practice-based dissertations that promote social justice. Experience in PK–12 teaching, teacher preparation, curriculum and instruction, multilingual education, and/or professional learning that would support teaching in undergraduate educator preparation programs. Ability to collaborate on program development and improvement. Strong interpersonal, collaboration, and organizational skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salary of $75,000 for Assistant Teaching Professor. However, our anticipated expected hiring salary is expected to be $90,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on qualifications, and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $100,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following required documents with your application: Comprehensive Curriculum Vitae (CV). Cover letter describing the candidate’s relevant professional and teaching experience; experience working with PK–12 educators and educational leaders; experience with graduate or doctoral education; and areas of expertise relevant to the position. One course syllabus relevant to courses that might be taught in the Ed.D. Leadership in Schooling program. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, which may include a summary of course evaluations, evaluations of professional development facilitated by the candidate, or an evaluation conducted by a supervisor. Names and contact information of three professional references will be required at the time of application. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530472/assistant-teaching-professor-of-teacher-education-and-ai-school-of-education": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Teacher Education and AI - School of Education",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Teacher Education and AI - School of Education Apply now Job no: 530472 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: School of Education Applications Open: Nov 10 2026 Applications Close: General Summary of Position: We seek a teacher educator and professional-learning leader with expertise in elementary education and a strong vision for how artificial intelligence and emerging digital technologies can be thoughtfully integrated into K–12 education and teacher preparation. We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute to elementary teacher preparation in areas such as mathematics and/or science and who bring knowledge or experience related to special education and inclusive teaching practices. The ideal candidate will bring practical experience working to strengthen teaching and learning through curriculum, instruction, educational technology, and emerging AI applications. The ideal candidate should be able to connect strong foundations in teaching and learning with rapidly evolving developments in AI and educational technology and translate these developments into meaningful educational practice. The successful candidate will teach primarily in the Bachelor of Arts in Education (BAEd) program while contributing, as appropriate, to other School of Education programs. Teaching responsibilities may include courses in elementary education, special education, math/science, educational technology and digital literacy, and other areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and program needs. The faculty member will also play a leadership role in updating digital literacy and educational technology preparation for preservice teachers, supporting curriculum development, developing professional-learning opportunities for practicing educators, and leading the conceptualization and development of new AI-related certificate or credential programs. We are particularly interested in candidates who view AI integration as more than learning to use individual tools. Successful candidates should demonstrate a thoughtful understanding of how AI may influence the design of instruction, student learning, assessment, teacher practice, and responsible and ethical technology use. Of particular interest are candidates who can connect emerging technologies with effective, inclusive, and developmentally appropriate teaching practices in elementary and K–12 educational settings. Position responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Teach primarily in the BAEd program, including courses in elementary education, including special education, math/science, digital literacy, educational technology, and other areas aligned with the faculty member’s expertise and program needs. The faculty member may also teach courses across other School of Education programs, as appropriate. Advise and mentor undergraduate BAEd students and support their development as effective, inclusive, and reflective educators prepared to teach in an evolving educational environment. Provide leadership in reviewing and strengthening the BAEd curriculum to ensure that preparation in AI, educational technology, and digital literacy is current, coherent, and integrated with strong foundations in curriculum, instruction, assessment, and inclusive teaching practices. Collaborate with BAEd faculty to support curriculum development and continuous program improvement consistent with relevant Massachusetts Department of Elementary and Secondary Education (DESE) expectations for educator preparation. As appropriate, collaborate with faculty in other School of Education programs to support curriculum development and professional learning related to AI, emerging technologies, and effective instructional practices. Contribute to the conceptualization and development of certificate, credential, or related professional-learning programs focused on AI in education, educational technology, digital literacy, or related areas. Develop and support professional-learning opportunities for practicing teachers and other educators related to AI, educational technology, curriculum and instruction, and other areas of expertise. Contribute to student recruitment, engagement, and retention efforts within BAEd and other relevant School of Education programs. Provide service to the BAEd program, Department of Curriculum and Instruction, School of Education, and College. Minimum Qualifications (Required): An earned Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Elementary Education, Special Education, Educational Technology, Curriculum and Instruction, Teacher Education, Learning Sciences, Instructional Technology, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Elementary classroom teaching experience or substantial experience working directly with elementary educators and schools through professional development, instructional support, coaching, curriculum development, or related activities. Demonstrated knowledge and experience in curriculum, instruction, assessment, and/or the preparation and professional learning of educators. Experience integrating educational technology, digital tools, and/or emerging technologies into teaching, learning, curriculum, or educator professional development. Demonstrated ability to contribute to curriculum development, program development, or other academic or professional-learning initiatives. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, educators, school leaders, and other educational partners. Experience supporting educators professional learning related to AI or educational technology. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in elementary education, with particular preference for candidates with expertise in elementary mathematics and/or science. Experience in special education, inclusive education, and/or supporting students with disabilities in elementary educational settings. Experience preparing, mentoring, or providing professional learning for preservice or practicing elementary teachers. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively within diverse groups on complex problems. Experience advising and mentoring undergraduate students. Experience developing or redesigning teacher-education curricula, particularly in elementary education, to incorporate educational technology, digital literacy, AI, or emerging instructional practices. Demonstrated leadership in educational technology, AI-supported teaching and learning, teacher professional development, or related educational initiatives. Experience designing and facilitating sustained professional learning for teachers or other educators beyond one-time workshops. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and represented by the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA. The anticipated salary range, pursuant to the applicable Collective Bargaining Agreement, lists minimum salary of $75,000 for Assistant Teaching Professor. However, our anticipated expected minimum salary is expected to be $90,000. The anticipated maximum salary, depending on qualifications, and departmental considerations, is not expected to exceed $100,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following documents with your application: Curriculum vitae. Cover letter: The cover letter should describe the applicant’s relevant experience and qualifications in elementary education and teacher preparation and articulate their vision for the thoughtful integration of artificial intelligence and emerging technologies into teaching and learning. Applicants are encouraged to describe relevant expertise in areas such as elementary mathematics and/or science, special or inclusive education, educational technology, curriculum development, and professional learning. Two samples of teaching or professional-learning materials that demonstrate the applicant’s experience in elementary education, teacher preparation, educational technology, digital literacy, AI in education, or related areas. Examples may include course syllabi, lesson or unit plans, workshop agendas, professional-development materials, or similar materials. Statement of diversity. Please describe your experiences, approaches, and/or commitments related to supporting students and educators from diverse backgrounds and creating inclusive and equitable learning environments. The statement may address your teaching, mentoring, curriculum development, professional learning, or work with schools and communities. Names and contact information for three professional references will be required at the time of application. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
  },
